WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.111 --> 00:00:10.657
<v Maya>You know, we live in this really bizarre technological moment right now. I mean, if you sit down at your laptop today, you can ask an AI to write a completely original symphony, right?</v>

2
00:00:11.237 --> 00:00:14.559
<v Daniel>Right. Or like generate a thousand lines of flawless Python code.</v>

3
00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:25.605
<v Maya>Exactly. And it does it in, what, three seconds? But then if you take this multimillion dollar state of the art robot and ask it to fold a pile of laundry.</v>

4
00:00:26.408 --> 00:00:28.669
<v Daniel>Oh, it fails. Yeah. Just completely fails.</v>

5
00:00:28.829 --> 00:00:31.711
<v Maya>Yeah. It gets totally confused by like a wrinkled sleeve.</v>

6
00:00:32.331 --> 00:00:47.300
<v Daniel>It's a really jarring contrast. We're seeing artificial intelligence conquer some of the most complex cognitive casks we can even dream up. But the physical world, it just remains incredibly stubborn. Yeah. Getting a machine to move through a messy physical</v>

7
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.743
<v Daniel>environment is, well, it's exponentially harder than getting software to navigate a database.</v>

8
00:00:53.396 --> 00:01:02.160
<v Maya>And that paradox, you know, why AI can think like a genius, but physical robots still stumble around like toddlers. That is the core of what we are unpacking in today's deep dive.</v>

9
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.401
<v Daniel>Yeah, it's a huge topic.</v>

10
00:01:03.801 --> 00:01:11.344
<v Maya>So our mission today for you listening is to take apart this brand new paper. It literally just published today, September 30th, 2026.</v>

11
00:01:11.425 --> 00:01:12.225
<v Daniel>Rush off the presses.</v>

12
00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:22.950
<v Maya>Right. It's by Russell Legate Yang and Maxim Masenkov, and they're from Anthropics Economics team. And the title just asks this wonderfully straightforward question, which is what work can robots do?</v>

13
00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:31.295
<v Daniel>Which is the question on everyone's mind. But to really answer that, we also brought in a stack of five older foundational economics papers from the last decade.</v>

14
00:01:31.315 --> 00:01:32.516
<v Maya>Right, because we need that context.</v>

15
00:01:32.616 --> 00:01:43.563
<v Daniel>Exactly. We want to see if this new anthropic research actually confirms all those old theories about automation or if, you know, the recent explosion in AI has just totally shattered our previous assumptions.</v>

16
00:01:44.038 --> 00:01:54.605
<v Maya>So let's jump right in. Let's start with that physical bottleneck. To figure out what a robot can actually do, the anthropic authors realized they first had to define where the robot is trying to do it, right?</v>

17
00:01:54.905 --> 00:02:01.970
<v Daniel>Yeah, because the environment is everything. And this traces directly back to one of our background sources, which is David Autor's 2015 paper.</v>

18
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.151
<v Maya>Oh, the Why Are There Still So Many Jobs paper?</v>

19
00:02:04.231 --> 00:02:13.377
<v Daniel>That's the one. Autor popularized this concept of environmental control, and he heavily relied on something called Polanyi's paradox.</v>

20
00:02:14.082 --> 00:02:16.007
<v Maya>I love Polanyi's paradox. It's</v>

21
00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:16.688
<v Daniel>fascinating.</v>

22
00:02:17.109 --> 00:02:31.840
<v Maya>It's basically this observation that human beings know far more than we can actually tell. Right. If you think about riding a bicycle, you just do it. You feel the balance. But if you try to sit down and write out the exact mathematical physics equations required to maintain that</v>

23
00:02:31.941 --> 00:02:45.051
<v Maya>equilibrium, you'd go crazy. And most human physical labor operates on that exact same intuition. We adapt to chaos naturally. But to automate a task, you have to map all that chaos into rigid rules.</v>

24
00:02:45.451 --> 00:02:59.918
<v Daniel>Which is why, historically, industrial automation has just been incredibly brittle. I mean, you can't just drop a robot into a messy room and tell it to figure things out. You have to meticulously engineer the entire environment around the machine. You remove</v>

25
00:03:00.318 --> 00:03:05.702
<v Daniel>every single unpredictable variable just so the robot can perform one repetitive motion.</v>

26
00:03:06.482 --> 00:03:08.644
<v Maya>It's like the consumer robot vacuum effect.</v>

27
00:03:08.744 --> 00:03:09.945
<v Daniel>Oh, perfect example.</v>

28
00:03:10.045 --> 00:03:16.048
<v Maya>Yeah. If you clear the floor, pick up your shoes, tuck away those rug fringes, your vacuum looks like an absolute genius.</v>

29
00:03:16.469 --> 00:03:18.250
<v Daniel>But if you let it loose in a cluttered garage?</v>

30
00:03:18.743 --> 00:03:21.564
<v Maya>It gets hopelessly stranded on a single stray extension cord.</v>

31
00:03:21.924 --> 00:03:32.609
<v Daniel>Exactly. So the anthropic researchers, they took that exact limitation and they operationalized it into a really strict rubric. They wanted real data, not just theories.</v>

32
00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:34.130
<v Maya>So how did they do that?</v>

33
00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:45.379
<v Daniel>Well, they started with O-Net, which is the standard US database of occupations. They filtered through about 900 jobs, which encompasses roughly 19,000 specific tasks.</v>

34
00:03:45.679 --> 00:03:46.100
<v Maya>Wow. OK.</v>

35
00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:48.903
<v Daniel>And from that, they isolated 7,594 tasks that are purely physical. OK.</v>

36
00:03:52.558 --> 00:04:07.223
<v Maya>So they have this massive list of physical tasks. But instead of just, you know, guessing if a machine could do them, they set the AI model Claude loose on the Internet. Right. They had Claude search for actual evidence of real deployed or demonstrated robots performing these actions.</v>

37
00:04:07.663 --> 00:04:13.045
<v Maya>And then they graded them on the scale from E0 to E3. Right. Based entirely on environmental control.</v>

38
00:04:13.598 --> 00:04:27.468
<v Daniel>Yeah, the scale is brilliant in its simplicity. So E0 just means the robot simply cannot do it, no matter the environment. Okay. Then E1 represents a purpose-built robotic environment. So imagine like an automotive assembly line.</v>

39
00:04:27.908 --> 00:04:31.051
<v Maya>Where the factory is basically a giant steel cage built around the machine.</v>

40
00:04:31.331 --> 00:04:36.795
<v Daniel>Exactly. Then E2 is a structured human workplace, like an Amazon logistics center.</v>

41
00:04:37.073 --> 00:04:44.217
<v Maya>Oh, right. So humans still work there, but it's highly standardized for machines. Flat floors, barcodes everywhere, super clear aisles.</v>

42
00:04:44.657 --> 00:04:49.599
<v Daniel>Spot on. And then finally, there is E3, the unstructured environment.</v>

43
00:04:49.879 --> 00:04:51.500
<v Maya>Like a busy city intersection.</v>

44
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.302
<v Daniel>Or a messy construction site. Or a chaotic hospital emergency room.</v>

45
00:04:56.583 --> 00:05:03.446
<v Maya>Right. So walk us through a concrete example. Like, how strict is this rubric when you apply it to a real job?</v>

46
00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:14.564
<v Daniel>Okay, the paper uses a great example, digging trenches. So if you run digging trenches through the model, Claude splits the job up by the amount of time each subtask takes.</v>

47
00:05:14.824 --> 00:05:15.564
<v Maya>Okay, makes sense.</v>

48
00:05:15.904 --> 00:05:29.549
<v Daniel>And Claude finds that cutting a simple linear trench in open ground takes about 25% of a worker's time. And because there are autonomous excavators doing that exact thing outdoors today, that specific action gets an E3 rating.</v>

49
00:05:29.989 --> 00:05:33.090
<v Maya>Because the hardware can actually handle the unstructured dirt.</v>

50
00:05:33.773 --> 00:05:46.440
<v Daniel>Right. But digging trenches isn't just mindlessly tearing up dirt. Another 20% of that exact same job involves careful, manual digging around buried pipes or, you know, delicate electrical conduit.</v>

51
00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.101
<v Maya>Oh, yeah. That requires tactile feedback.</v>

52
00:05:49.381 --> 00:05:55.885
<v Daniel>Exactly. A human worker feels the resistance of a PVC pipe through the handle of their shovel, and they instantly stop pushing.</v>

53
00:05:56.215 --> 00:05:59.998
<v Maya>But a multi-ton excavator doesn't feel that. It just rips the pipe right in half.</v>

54
00:06:00.158 --> 00:06:11.547
<v Daniel>Yeah, it destroys it. So when Claude searches for a robot with the fine motor skills to gently navigate unknown buried utilities, it finds zero evidence. That subtask gets slapped with an E0.</v>

55
00:06:12.363 --> 00:06:16.624
<v Maya>Wow. So the lack of tactile feedback completely breaks the capability.</v>

56
00:06:16.944 --> 00:06:26.727
<v Daniel>It really does. And because the paper uses a majority rule methodology based on time, the delicate, impossible work outweighs the easy linear digging.</v>

57
00:06:28.228 --> 00:06:28.788
<v Maya>Ah, I see.</v>

58
00:06:29.428 --> 00:06:35.550
<v Daniel>So therefore, the overall job of trench digging is ultimately classified by Anthropic as unexposed to robotics.</v>

59
00:06:36.057 --> 00:06:45.479
<v Maya>That is a brutal filter. But when they ran all 7,594 physical tasks through it, the numbers they came out with were still massive.</v>

60
00:06:45.919 --> 00:06:46.540
<v Daniel>They really were.</v>

61
00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:56.362
<v Maya>I mean, technically speaking, robots currently have the capability to perform 74% of physical tasks. That maps out to 34% of all U.S. working time.</v>

62
00:06:56.842 --> 00:07:11.675
<v Daniel>But, and this is a huge but, the devil is in the distribution. You have to ask where that 74% capability actually lives on the rubric. Right. The vast majority of it is locked in E1 and E2, those highly controlled settings. When you look at</v>

63
00:07:11.715 --> 00:07:20.324
<v Daniel>tasks that require E3 capability operating in the messy real world, robots can only handle 1% of all physical tasks.</v>

64
00:07:20.724 --> 00:07:35.422
<v Maya>Wait, 1%? Just 1%. So we have this massive technical capability, but its actual footprint in the unstructured real world is just a sliver. Still, though, 74 percent in controlled environments is huge. It really begs the question,</v>

65
00:07:35.542 --> 00:07:37.463
<v Maya>who is standing in the crosshairs here?</v>

66
00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:44.425
<v Daniel>Well, if you sort their index by the most exposed occupations, nine of the top 10 are vehicle operators.</v>

67
00:07:44.925 --> 00:07:45.305
<v Maya>Really?</v>

68
00:07:45.845 --> 00:07:51.807
<v Daniel>Yeah. Taxi drivers actually take the number one spot across the entire U.S. economy with a score of 2.2 out of three.</v>

69
00:07:52.945 --> 00:08:04.335
<v Maya>You know, that actually makes perfect logical sense. A road is technically an unstructured E3 environment because of weather and pedestrians, but driving itself is a strictly rules-based system.</v>

70
00:08:04.575 --> 00:08:04.975
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

71
00:08:05.055 --> 00:08:18.467
<v Maya>You stay between the painted lines, you stop at the red octagon, yield to the crosswalk. It's chaos, but it's highly regulated chaos. Plus, I mean, we've poured hundreds of billions of dollars into autonomous vehicle research to solve that exact problem.</v>

72
00:08:19.122 --> 00:08:29.826
<v Daniel>We have. But we can't just look at physical robots in a vacuum. To get the full picture of the workforce, we have to layer this new physical data on top of the cognitive automation we mentioned at the start.</v>

73
00:08:30.446 --> 00:08:30.786
<v Maya>Oh, right.</v>

74
00:08:31.226 --> 00:08:41.530
<v Daniel>And this is where two more of our background sources come in. There's a 2024 paper by Alundu and colleagues and an early 2026 paper by Massinkoff and McCrory.</v>

75
00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.251
<v Maya>And both of those studies looked at large language models, right?</v>

76
00:08:44.653 --> 00:08:52.840
<v Daniel>Right. They measured how exposed jobs are to LLMs. And they found that LLMs alone expose about half of all work in the economy.</v>

77
00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:54.641
<v Maya>Which is already a huge number.</v>

78
00:08:55.162 --> 00:09:01.867
<v Daniel>But when you stack Anthropic's new physical robot data on top of that LLM data, the combined number is just staggering.</v>

79
00:09:02.067 --> 00:09:02.828
<v Maya>It jumps to</v>

80
00:09:03.669 --> 00:09:13.937
<v Daniel>81%. 81%. So between software that processes information and hardware that manipulates objects, four-fifths of all human work tasks have some vulnerability right now.</v>

81
00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:25.237
<v Maya>It's massive, but the demographics of who is exposed reveal a really profound split. The worker whose job is threatened by a robot is the statistical mirror opposite of the worker whose job is threatened by an LLM.</v>

82
00:09:25.598 --> 00:09:25.818
<v Daniel>Yes.</v>

83
00:09:26.378 --> 00:09:41.188
<v Maya>This demographic flip is arguably the most fascinating part of the data for me. Because if you are in a job highly exposed to LLMs, you likely hold a bachelor's degree, you work in an office, and you have a relatively high income. Right. But if you look at the jobs exposed to</v>

84
00:09:41.308 --> 00:09:56.154
<v Maya>physical robots, those workers are a massive 55 points less likely to hold a bachelor's degree. 55 points. And they are less likely to be female. They earn on average $30 an hour less than the LLM-exposed worker. And they</v>

85
00:09:56.234 --> 00:09:59.255
<v Maya>already face more than twice the baseline unemployment rate.</v>

86
00:09:59.695 --> 00:10:11.445
<v Daniel>It creates this distinct bifurcation. The cognitive wave is just washing over the white-collar, highly educated office worker, while the physical wave is grinding toward the blue-collar manual laborer.</v>

87
00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:26.994
<v Maya>OK, so we have this 81 percent exposure footprint. But, you know, exposure is just a theoretical vulnerability. It doesn't mean your boss is firing you tomorrow. Exactly. To understand if that exposure turns into actual job loss, we have to talk about money. We have to pivot from</v>

88
00:10:27.034 --> 00:10:31.055
<v Maya>what a robot can do to what a business will actually pay a robot to do.</v>

89
00:10:31.475 --> 00:10:40.637
<v Daniel>Which brings us to a critical tension in labor economics. And this is perfectly illustrated by another one of our background sources, Frey and Osborne's infamous 2017 paper.</v>

90
00:10:41.738 --> 00:10:43.520
<v Maya>Oh, that paper caused absolute panic.</v>

91
00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:52.348
<v Daniel>It really did. They predicted that 47 percent of U.S. jobs were at high risk of computerization. But their entire model was built on capability alone.</v>

92
00:10:52.789 --> 00:10:58.274
<v Maya>Right. They essentially argued the technology exists to do this task. Therefore, this job will disappear.</v>

93
00:10:58.614 --> 00:11:06.782
<v Daniel>Yes. And the new anthropic paper pushes back on that violently. They insist that capability is an illusion if the economics don't make sense.</v>

94
00:11:07.548 --> 00:11:09.928
<v Maya>It's like commuting to work in a private helicopter.</v>

95
00:11:10.108 --> 00:11:11.269
<v Daniel>Right. Exactly.</v>

96
00:11:11.409 --> 00:11:24.611
<v Maya>Like, is the helicopter technically capable of moving you three miles to the office and avoiding traffic? Sure. But the fuel, the pilot, the maintenance, it's economically absurd. You're just going to drive your car because the car is cheaper.</v>

97
00:11:24.811 --> 00:11:35.313
<v Daniel>That is the perfect analogy. Because when the anthropic researchers actually ran the cost analysis on deploying robots, the results completely deflated that Frey and Osborne panic.</v>

98
00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:37.470
<v Maya>So what were the numbers?</v>

99
00:11:37.951 --> 00:11:50.754
<v Daniel>Well, remember that 74% capability number? When you factor in the cost of buying, programming, and maintaining the hardware, robots are only cost competitive for 0.3% of job tasks today. Wait, 0.3%? 0.3%. That isn't a robotic</v>

100
00:11:51.454 --> 00:12:06.314
<v Maya>takeover. That is a rounding error. It really is. So let's break down the mechanics of this. Where does the math actually work and where does it fall apart? Where is</v>

101
00:12:06.354 --> 00:12:08.214
<v Maya>that 0.3% happening?</v>

102
00:12:08.794 --> 00:12:23.518
<v Daniel>The clearest economic win right now is in packing and packaging. If you are putting standardized items into cardboard boxes, the robots are winning. A logistics company can buy a $2 million automated packaging installation that replaces roughly 14</v>

103
00:12:24.178 --> 00:12:24.799
<v Daniel>human workers.</v>

104
00:12:25.399 --> 00:12:29.940
<v Maya>Okay, but $2 million is a massive upfront capital expenditure.</v>

105
00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:45.499
<v Daniel>It is, but the mechanism of the savings justifies it. Because boxes are standardized. The machine doesn't have to think very hard. It just repeats a geometry problem over and over. Right. So when you amortize the cost of that $2 million machine</v>

106
00:12:45.659 --> 00:12:55.965
<v Daniel>over its lifespan, the company ends up saving about $2,500 a year for every human worker it replaced. It's a slim margin, but at scale, it's highly profitable.</v>

107
00:12:56.712 --> 00:13:03.656
<v Maya>Okay, so standardization is the key there. What about a job where the robot fails the cost test miserably?</v>

108
00:13:04.317 --> 00:13:06.238
<v Daniel>Welding is the perfect counterexample.</v>

109
00:13:06.578 --> 00:13:07.119
<v Maya>Oh, interesting.</v>

110
00:13:07.399 --> 00:13:22.128
<v Daniel>We absolutely have AI-powered robotic welders. Right. They are highly capable. But a human welder just needs a protective mask, a welding torch, and visual instructions. They can walk up to a custom joint, adjust their angle, and fuse the metal.</v>

111
00:13:22.548 --> 00:13:24.109
<v Maya>Right. They just adapt on the fly.</v>

112
00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:38.343
<v Daniel>Exactly. To get a robot to do that same weld, you need an engineer to CAD model the exact spatial path. You have to build a massive custom metal jig to hold the pieces within a millimeter of tolerance so the robot doesn't miss.</v>

113
00:13:38.823 --> 00:13:39.043
<v Maya>Wow.</v>

114
00:13:39.323 --> 00:13:41.906
<v Daniel>And you have to build safety enclosures around the whole setup.</v>

115
00:13:42.577 --> 00:13:49.959
<v Maya>So the overhead isn't just the robot arm. It's the entire infrastructure required to make the robot's blind environment predictable?</v>

116
00:13:50.259 --> 00:14:02.583
<v Daniel>Which is exactly why a robotic welding setup ends up costing about five times more than simply hiring a human being. The human brain and hand are infinitely cheaper and more adaptable than building a custom jig for every new task.</v>

117
00:14:03.003 --> 00:14:10.965
<v Maya>That makes total sense. But wait, what about the taxi drivers? They topped the exposure index. And we see autonomous taxis driving around certain cities right now.</v>

118
00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:26.122
<v Daniel>Robotaxis are actually hovering right at cost parity. The hardware has gotten cheaper and the software is capable. But the paper points out that cost isn't the only barrier. Regulation blocks robots from performing about 14% of the physical tasks they're</v>

119
00:14:26.142 --> 00:14:26.743
<v Daniel>capable of.</v>

120
00:14:27.423 --> 00:14:42.351
<v Maya>Of course. A city council can just vote to ban autonomous vehicles from their streets, regardless of how cheap they are. Exactly. So we have these massive barricades protecting physical labor. The paper notes that pure capability, largely the lack of human-level manual</v>

121
00:14:42.391 --> 00:14:57.055
<v Maya>dexterity, blocks 70% of tasks. Right. Regulation blocks another 14%. And then human preference blocks another 25%. I mean, even if a robotic daycare worker is cheap and safe, no parent wants a machine dressing their toddler. We demand a</v>

122
00:14:57.095 --> 00:14:57.395
<v Maya>human.</v>

123
00:14:57.675 --> 00:15:12.591
<v Daniel>We absolutely do. So given all those barricades, the authors decided to map out a timeline. Historically, the cost of robotics hardware declines by about 3% every year. If you project that 3% decline forward, the math</v>

124
00:15:12.671 --> 00:15:18.801
<v Daniel>says it'll take 40 years for robots to become cost-competitive for just 10% of physical work.</v>

125
00:15:19.452 --> 00:15:27.974
<v Maya>40 years just to hit 10%. That means we wouldn't see robots taking over half of all physical work until the year 2085.</v>

126
00:15:28.234 --> 00:15:36.997
<v Daniel>Right. And even in their most aggressive fast-track scenario where hardware prices just plummet, half of the work still isn't cost-competitive until 2050.</v>

127
00:15:38.122 --> 00:15:43.264
<v Maya>But forecasting technology three to six decades into the future is inherently risky, right?</v>

128
00:15:43.544 --> 00:15:56.709
<v Daniel>Very risky. So to validate those distant timelines, the authors did something really clever. They ran a back test. They took their exact same rubric and applied it to the robotics technology available back in the year 1977. Oh,</v>

129
00:15:57.549 --> 00:15:58.209
<v Maya>that's smart.</v>

130
00:15:58.609 --> 00:16:06.592
<v Daniel>Yeah. And then they tracked what actually happened to the wages and employment of the jobs most exposed to that 1970s tech over the subsequent decades.</v>

131
00:16:07.032 --> 00:16:08.493
