132
00:16:09.254 --> 00:16:20.625
<v Daniel>It showed a slow, grinding decline. Jobs highly exposed to 1977 robots did eventually suffer wage stagnation and employment drops, but it took years to materialize.</v>

133
00:16:21.025 --> 00:16:21.425
<v Maya>Wow.</v>

134
00:16:21.946 --> 00:16:29.413
<v Daniel>They calculated that, historically, robots learn how to do about 2% of the physical work they previously couldn't do every single year.</v>

135
00:16:30.009 --> 00:16:38.720
<v Maya>You know, this historical backtest perfectly aligns with our final background source, Duran Esamoglu and Pascual Restrepo's 2020 paper, Robots and Jobs.</v>

136
00:16:39.341 --> 00:16:40.583
<v Daniel>Yes, exactly.</v>

137
00:16:41.133 --> 00:16:55.959
<v Maya>They looked at specific U.S. commuting zones and found that for every single additional industrial robot introduced per 1,000 workers, employment and wages locally dropped. So this anthropic backtest confirms the Acemoglu Restrepo data. Automation</v>

138
00:16:56.059 --> 00:17:01.862
<v Maya>does destroy specific jobs, but it is a slow, hyper-local bleed, not an overnight apocalypse.</v>

139
00:17:02.388 --> 00:17:12.624
<v Daniel>The data is really robust. But, you know, we have to address the elephants in the room here. There are several major tensions in this anthropic paper that require some critical pushback.</v>

140
00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:28.152
<v Maya>I am so glad you brought that up because I have some serious issues with how this study is constructed. First of all, the entire index relies on Claude, an LLM, rating the tasks, scraping the web, and estimating the costs of industrial hardware. We are literally asking</v>

141
00:17:28.192 --> 00:17:42.663
<v Maya>an AI to grade its own homework. Pricing data for enterprise robotics is notoriously opaque. Companies don't just list a $2 million packaging machine on a website with a buy now button. How much can we really trust an AI's judgment on nuanced physical capabilities and hidden costs?</v>

142
00:17:43.121 --> 00:17:57.838
<v Daniel>It is a profound limitation. LLMs hallucinate, and they definitely struggle with reasoning about physical physics. Right. The authors try to mitigate this by having Claude cite every single source it used to prove a robot exists and can do the task.</v>

143
00:17:58.518 --> 00:18:07.883
<v Daniel>But at the end of the day, using software to evaluate the limitations of hardware introduces a layer of abstraction that might obscure the truth on the factory floor.</v>

144
00:18:08.123 --> 00:18:18.067
<v Maya>Yeah, exactly. And the second tension for me is the reliance on the back test. Forecasting the future based on a linear 2% learning rate from 1977 feels dangerous.</v>

145
00:18:18.368 --> 00:18:19.048
